---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Pass the Solihull PHV Test: 2026 Guide"
  description="How to pass the Solihull PHV test: 48 questions in 60 minutes, a 36/48 pass mark, the six subjects covered, and how Solihull differs from Wolverhampton."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>How to Pass the Solihull PHV Test: 2026 Guide</h1>

      <p>
        This guide explains <strong>how to pass the Solihull PHV test</strong>
        — the knowledge test you sit to get a private hire or hackney carriage
        driver licence from Solihull Metropolitan Borough Council. It covers
        the format, the six subjects, the pass mark, how Solihull differs from
        Wolverhampton, and how to prepare. Everything here is drawn from the
        council's own published guidance, linked at the bottom of the page.
      </p>

      <h2>What is the Solihull knowledge test?</h2>

      <p>
        In Solihull the knowledge test is not a standalone exam you book on its
        own — it comes at the end of a mandatory training course. The course
        runs for approximately <strong>4 hours</strong> (with breaks), and the
        test follows it with an extra <strong>60 minutes</strong> allowed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The questions are a <strong>combination of spoken, written and
        multiple-choice</strong>, so it is worth being able to explain an
        answer, not just recognise it. Results are issued
        <strong>within 5 working days</strong>, and your certificate needs to
        be less than 12 months old when you apply for your licence.
      </p>

      <h2>The Solihull test format</h2>

      <p>
        The paper is evenly balanced — there are
        <strong>48 questions, 8 on each of the six subjects</strong>, and you
        need <strong>36 correct (75%)</strong> to pass.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Element</th>
            <th>Solihull</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Questions</td>
            <td>48 (8 per subject)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Time allowed</td>
            <td>60 minutes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pass mark</td>
            <td>36 out of 48 (75%)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Key questions</td>
            <td>None — the paper is marked on overall score</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Format</td>
            <td>Spoken, written and multiple-choice</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Results</td>
            <td>Within 5 working days</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What the six subjects cover</h2>

      <p>
        The course and the test are built on the same six subjects, and each
        one carries exactly 8 questions. That even split matters for revision:
        no single topic can be safely skipped, because each is worth the same
        as any other.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Safeguarding</strong> — including understanding Child Sexual
          Abuse and Exploitation (CSAE) risks and county lines exploitation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Disability awareness</strong> and the Equality Act 2010.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Customer service</strong> and communication and language
          skills.
        </li>
        <li><strong>The Highway Code</strong> and road safety.</li>
        <li><strong>Vehicle maintenance.</strong></li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compliance</strong> with legislation, conditions and policy
          requirements.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How Solihull differs from Wolverhampton</h2>

      <p>
        Drivers often assume the two are interchangeable. They are not — the
        papers are different sizes, different lengths, and marked under
        different rules. A pass with one council does not qualify you with the
        other.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Solihull</th>
            <th>Wolverhampton</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Questions</td>
            <td>48</td>
            <td>28</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Time</td>
            <td>60 minutes</td>
            <td>45 minutes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pass mark</td>
            <td>36 out of 48 (75%)</td>
            <td>21 out of 28 (75%)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Key questions</td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>8 — one wrong answer fails the paper</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Subjects</td>
            <td>6, evenly weighted</td>
            <td>7</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        The single biggest practical difference is the absence of key
        questions. In Wolverhampton, one wrong answer in the wrong category
        fails an otherwise strong paper — see our guide on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-pass/"
          >passing the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >
        if that is the council you are applying to. In Solihull, every
        question carries the same weight, so what matters is a consistent
        spread across all six subjects rather than one perfect category.
      </p>

      <h2>How to prepare</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Spread your revision evenly.</strong> With 8 questions per
          subject, being strong in four areas and weak in two is exactly how
          people land just under 36.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Practise under timed conditions.</strong> 48 questions in 60
          minutes is a comfortable pace if you know the material — the risk is
          rushing and misreading, not the clock itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Be able to explain your answers.</strong> Part of the test is
          spoken, so understanding why an answer is right matters more than
          memorising the option letter.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review every mistake.</strong> Track which of the six
          subjects you drop marks in, and revise that subject specifically.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        The <strong>PHV Prep UK</strong> app has a dedicated Solihull mode: it
        builds mock papers the way Solihull does — 48 questions, 8 from each of
        the six subjects, marked at 36/48 — and tracks your score by subject so
        you can see exactly where the marks are going. Download it on
        <a
          href="https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/phv-prep-uk-taxi-badge-test/id6776364932"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">the App Store</a
        >
        or
        <a
          href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.phvprepuk.app&pcampaignid=web_share"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Google Play</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>What if I fail?</h2>

      <p>
        Solihull states that if you do not pass, you may retake the whole
        course or just the test, and that a fee will be payable. The council
        does not publish that fee on its driver licence guide, so check the
        current amount with the licensing team before you rebook.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>

      <p>
        Ready to start the process? Read
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/solihull/how-to-apply/"
          >how to apply for a Solihull PHV licence</a
        >
        for the full step-by-step, or see the
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/solihull/faqs/"
          >Solihull PHV test FAQs</a
        >
        for quick answers. You can also learn more about the app on the
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk">PHV Prep UK page</a>.
      </p>

